# __NAME__

A thin OpenCapture camera app, generated by `atlas new-app` from OpenCine
(`atlas/templates/app`). It composes OpenCine's shared app shell (`OpenCineAppKit` on iOS, the
`appkit` module on Android) with a brand and an integration list. Shared behavior belongs in
OpenCine, never here: fix it there and move this app's pin.

| Path | What |
|---|---|
| `opencine-pins.txt`, `scripts/checkout-opencine.sh` | The exact OpenCine commit this app builds on, checked out as `../OpenCine` |
| `ios/project.yml` | XcodeGen spec: `__NAME__` (ships), `__NAME__Simulated` (developer-only), `__NAME__Tests` |
| `ios/__NAME__/App.swift`, `Shell.swift` | Brand, shell composition, the monitor and cover slots |
| `ios/__NAME__/Integrations.swift` | The integrations the app ships |
| `ios/__NAME__/__NAME__.entitlements` | Entitlements (empty: add what your integration needs) |
| `ios/Simulated/`, `ios/Tests/` | SIMULATED integration list and the shell contract test |
| `Android/app/src/main` | The same composition in Compose |
| `Android/app/src/camera`, `src/simulated`, `src/testSimulated` | Integration list per flavor, contract test |
| `ios/TestFlight/`, `Android/Play/` | Tester notes for each build (CI checks them) |
| `.github/workflows/ci.yml`, `release.yml` | CI on every change; a manual release build of the shipping variants |

## Build

```sh
scripts/checkout-opencine.sh                     # once; OpenCine next to this checkout
(cd ios && xcodegen generate)
xcodebuild -project ios/__NAME__.xcodeproj -scheme __NAME__Simulated \
  -destination 'generic/platform=iOS Simulator' build
(cd Android && ./gradlew test assembleSimulatedDebug assembleCameraRelease)
```

CI fetches the pinned OpenCine commit from GitHub, so it passes once that commit is pushed.

## SIMULATED variants

`__NAME__Simulated` (iOS) and the `simulated` flavor (Android) list OpenCine's `FakeIntegration`
from its test support: one nearby camera that every connect reaches. They exist so the shell
runs and the contract test passes without hardware. They are developer-only: they prove
composition, not any camera, and the release workflow never builds them.

## Adding a real integration

List it in `ios/__NAME__/Integrations.swift` and `Android/app/src/camera/.../Integrations.kt`
with its typed slots (`AppIntegration(integration, slots: ...)`), link its OpenCine product
(`ios/project.yml`) or module (`Android/settings.gradle.kts` substitution and
`app/build.gradle.kts`), and add the entitlements and usage strings it needs. An integration that
needs an app-side port still lives partly in the existing apps; move that port into OpenCine
first.
